  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 715 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nEarly Thanksgiving Snow Potential South of Highway 18\n\nThe ingredients for what is shaping up to be an interesting \nThanksgiving winter storm are coming together across the Midwest\ntonight. An expansive mid to upper level moisture plume ahead \nof a northern stream low currently diving down the Missouri \nRiver is providing us with the current fetch of high clouds (and\nlight snow over the Siouxland). The low works into the lower \nMissouri River Valley over the next 24 hours and slows as a \nsouthern stream shortwave counterpart rotates cyclonically \naround the mean longwave trough base, across the northern Gulf \nand eventually up the Eastern Seaboard for Thursday. A north-to-\nsouth inverted near-surface trough sets up within a region of \npotent kinematic and isentropic ascent for Wednesday night into \nThursday, potentially yielding an impressive early season \nsnowstorm focused in western Illinois. \n\nThe base of this inverted trough pivots through the region \nduring this time. Its passage looks to be transient and of \nquestionable moisture content, but there still exists a roughly \n10% probability for measurable snow--less than an inch--along \nand south of Highway 18 (or a Nashua, IA to Dodgeville, WI \nline). These probabilities ramp up steadily as one travels south\nof Dubuque with model histograms over east-central Iowa showing\na bimodal distribution indicative of the uncertainties in the \npotent band placement.\n\nFor the local area, with model guidance (led by the AI \nsolutions and long range CAMS) trending south, have tempered the\nmessaging on any impacts from snow. However, the impacts do \nincrease quickly as one travels just south of the area.\n\n\nWeekend Snow Potential\n\nShortwave ridging moves over the region for Friday with zonal flow \nfor Saturday. A weak front passes through the Upper Midwest Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, bringing about a low chance for \nsnow or rain showers with temperatures around the freezing mark.\nEnsemble and deterministic models support a shortwave coming \ndown Sunday; however, there are differences in the strength of \nthis wave and attendant moisture. Of the available LREF guidance\nat that range, only about 10-20% of the members have any \naccumulating precipitation with these weekend systems. \n\n\nTemperature Trends through the Weekend\n\nAn elongated Great Lakes high pressure system remains the \ndominant surface feature in play through Friday, with a mean N \nto NE fetch keeping temperatures near seasonal norms. That being\nsaid, the ridge axis pivots cyclonically over the region for \nthe end of the week as our Thanksgiving system lifts up the \nEastern Seaboard. Lower tropospheric warm air advection takes \nplace Thursday as the warm sector responsible for the snow to \nthe south pivots around the aforementioned upper level low. \nHowever, a continued northerly surface fetch tempers the degree\nof warming we see with this modified maritime tropical airmass \nand highs only look to warm a few degrees between Wednesday and \nThursday. \n\nThe ridge orients meridionally for late Friday and the surface \nflow turns more southwesterly, ushering in a weak ribbon of \npositive theta-e advection late Friday into Saturday. Increasing\nsun for Friday should push temperatures into the mid-40s with \nthe warmest day on Saturday as the clearer skies and warmer \nboundary-layer temperatures align to produce highs in the \nmid-40s to near 50. Temperatures roll back 5 to 10 degrees for\nthe start of next week in the wake of our weekend frontal\npassages.",
